    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`, and all 7 tests pass (the 3 that were there plus 4 new ones).\n\n```python\nasync def run_tool(session, name, arguments):\n    result = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\n    if result.is_error:\n        raise ToolCallFailed(name, _text_of(result))\n    if result.structured_content is not None:\n        return result.structured_content\n    return _text_of(result)\n```\n\n`_text_of` joins the text blocks with `\"\\n\"` and skips images and other non-text blocks. The structured-output check uses `is not None` rather than a truthiness test: in this mcp version structured output can be any JSON value, so a tool returning `0`, `[]` or `false` still gets its structured value back.\n\n**New tests:** `tests/test_calls.py` runs `run_tool` against a real in-memory MCP server. It covers structured output, a falsy structured value, joining several text blocks, and a failed call.\n\nTwo server behaviours you'll see in practice:\n- **Error text includes a prefix.** The failure message is the server's text exactly, so a tool raising `ToolError(\"no such version\")` gives `ToolCallFailed.message == \"Error executing tool deploy: no such version\"`. If a tool crashes with any other exception, the server sends only `\"Error executing tool <name>\"` and hides the original message.\n- **Catch errors inside the client context.** A `ToolCallFailed` that escapes an `async with Client(...)` block comes out wrapped in an ExceptionGroup. The CLI already catches it inside the session, so it works as written.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** my request to delete a scratch file was declined, so `.scratch/check.py` is still there. It's safe to delete it."
